Traditional operating systems use Access Control Lists. A process has a user ID, and the kernel checks that user ID against file permissions. The problem is ambient authority, a process can attempt to access any resource, and the kernel decides yes or no after the fact. This means a compromised process inherits all the permissions of its user.
Capability-based security inverts this. Instead of checking "who are you and what are you allowed to do?", the runtime says "here are handles to exactly the resources you can use, and nothing else exists." If you don't have a handle to the filesystem, you can't even attempt to open a file. There's no `open("/etc/passwd")` that returns "permission denied", the function `open` simply doesn't exist in your universe.
WASI Preview 2 implements this through the Component Model and WIT (WebAssembly Interface Types):
```wit
// This is a WIT world definition, it defines what a plugin can do
world data-transformer {
// What the plugin receives from the host
import wasi:io/streams@0.2.1;
import wasi:filesystem/types@0.2.1;
import wasi:filesystem/preopens@0.2.1;
import wasi:logging/logging@0.1.0;
// What the plugin must implement
export transform: func(input: list<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, string>;
}
```
A component compiled against this world literally cannot make HTTP requests, because `wasi:http` isn't in its imports. It can't access the clock, can't spawn threads, can't open sockets. The Wasm runtime enforces this at link time, if a component tries to import an interface that isn't in its world, instantiation fails before a single instruction executes.
## Filesystem Sandboxing: Preopens and Directory Handles
WASI's filesystem model deserves special attention because it's where most plugin systems fail. In WASI Preview 2, a component doesn't get access to a filesystem, it gets handles to specific directories that the host "preopens":
```rust
// Host-side: configuring what the plugin can see
use wasmtime::component::*;
use wasmtime_wasi::{WasiCtxBuilder, DirPerms, FilePerms};
let mut wasi_ctx = WasiCtxBuilder::new();
// Plugin gets read-only access to /data/input
wasi_ctx.preopened_dir(
"/data/input",
"input",
DirPerms::READ,
FilePerms::READ,
)?;
// Plugin gets read-write access to /data/output
wasi_ctx.preopened_dir(
"/data/output",
"output",
DirPerms::all(),
FilePerms::all(),
)?;
// That's it. No other filesystem access exists.
```

Preview 1 used a POSIX-like API with file descriptors. Preview 2 replaces this with the Component Model, where capabilities are expressed as typed interfaces (WIT). Instead of file descriptor numbers, you get strongly-typed handles to specific resources. It's also composable, you can virtualize any capability by interposing a component.
### Can a WASI component escape its sandbox?
Not through the WASI API surface. A component can only access capabilities that were explicitly granted by the host at instantiation time.

